@anon.9efbc0c128adab6190fabe7f7e045142.850 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [1158 x i8] }> <{ [1158 x i8] c"Reads structured data from a JSON file.\0A\0AThe file must contain a valid JSON value, such as object or array. JSON\0Aobjects will be converted into Typst dictionaries, and JSON arrays will be\0Aconverted into Typst arrays. Strings and booleans will be converted into the\0ATypst equivalents, `null` will be converted into `{none}`, and numbers will\0Abe converted to floats or integers depending on whether they are whole\0Anumbers.\0A\0ABe aware that integers larger than 2<sup>63</sup>-1 will be converted to\0Afloating point numbers, which may result in an approximative value.\0A\0AThe function returns a dictionary, an array or, depending on the JSON file,\0Aanother JSON data type.\0A\0AThe JSON files in the example contain objects with the keys `temperature`,\0A`unit`, and `weather`.\0A\0A# Example\0A```example\0A#let forecast(day) = block[\0A  #box(square(\0A    width: 2cm,\0A    inset: 8pt,\0A    fill: if day.weather == \22sunny\22 {\0A      yellow\0A    } else {\0A      aqua\0A    },\0A    align(\0A      bottom + right,\0A      strong(day.weather),\0A    ),\0A  ))\0A  #h(6pt)\0A  #set text(22pt, baseline: -8pt)\0A  #day.temperature \C2\B0#day.unit\0A]\0A\0A#forecast(json(\22monday.json\22))\0A#forecast(json(\22tuesday.json\22))\0A```" }>, align 1
