@anon.9efbc0c128adab6190fabe7f7e045142.975 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [101 x i8] }> <{ [101 x i8] c"How sharp turns are rendered.\0A\0AIf set to `{auto}`, the value is inherited, defaulting to `{\22miter\22}`." }>, align 1
@anon.9efbc0c128adab6190fabe7f7e045142.976 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [916 x i8] }> <{ [916 x i8] c"The dash pattern to use. This can be:\0A\0A- One of the predefined patterns:\0A  - `{\22solid\22}` or `{none}`\0A  - `{\22dotted\22}`\0A  - `{\22densely-dotted\22}`\0A  - `{\22loosely-dotted\22}`\0A  - `{\22dashed\22}`\0A  - `{\22densely-dashed\22}`\0A  - `{\22loosely-dashed\22}`\0A  - `{\22dash-dotted\22}`\0A  - `{\22densely-dash-dotted\22}`\0A  - `{\22loosely-dash-dotted\22}`\0A- An [array] with alternating lengths for dashes and gaps. You can\0A  also use the string `{\22dot\22}` for a length equal to the line\0A  thickness.\0A- A [dictionary] with the keys `array` (same as the array above),\0A  and `phase` (of type [length]), which defines where in the pattern\0A  to start drawing.\0A\0AIf set to `{auto}`, the value is inherited, defaulting to `{none}`.\0A\0A```example\0A#set line(length: 100%, stroke: 2pt)\0A#stack(\0A  spacing: 1em,\0A  line(stroke: (dash: \22dashed\22)),\0A  line(stroke: (dash: (10pt, 5pt, \22dot\22, 5pt))),\0A  line(stroke: (dash: (array: (10pt, 5pt, \22dot\22, 5pt), phase: 10pt))),\0A)\0A```" }>, align 1
@anon.9efbc0c128adab6190fabe7f7e045142.977 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [687 x i8] }> <{ [687 x i8] c"Number at which protruding sharp bends are rendered with a bevel\0Ainstead or a miter join. The higher the number, the sharper an angle\0Acan be before it is bevelled. Only applicable if `join` is\0A`{\22miter\22}`.\0A\0ASpecifically, the miter limit is the maximum ratio between the\0Acorner's protrusion length and the stroke's thickness.\0A\0AIf set to `{auto}`, the value is inherited, defaulting to `{4.0}`.\0A\0A```example\0A#let points = ((15pt, 0pt), (0pt, 30pt), (30pt, 30pt), (10pt, 20pt))\0A#set path(stroke: 6pt + blue)\0A#stack(\0A    dir: ltr,\0A    spacing: 1cm,\0A    path(stroke: (miter-limit: 1), ..points),\0A    path(stroke: (miter-limit: 4), ..points),\0A    path(stroke: (miter-limit: 5), ..points),\0A)\0A```" }>, align 1
@anon.9efbc0c128adab6190fabe7f7e045142.978 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [6 x i8] }> <{ [6 x i8] c"Stroke" }>, align 1
@anon.9efbc0c128adab6190fabe7f7e045142.979 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [1453 x i8] }> <{ [1453 x i8] c"Defines how to draw a line.\0A\0AA stroke has a _paint_ (a solid color or gradient), a _thickness,_ a line\0A_cap,_ a line _join,_ a _miter limit,_ and a _dash_ pattern. All of these\0Avalues are optional and have sensible defaults.\0A\0A# Example\0A```example\0A#set line(length: 100%)\0A#stack(\0A  spacing: 1em,\0A  line(stroke: 2pt + red),\0A  line(stroke: (paint: blue, thickness: 4pt, cap: \22round\22)),\0A  line(stroke: (paint: blue, thickness: 1pt, dash: \22dashed\22)),\0A  line(stroke: 2pt + gradient.linear(..color.map.rainbow)),\0A)\0A```\0A\0A# Simple strokes\0AYou can create a simple solid stroke from a color, a thickness, or a\0Acombination of the two. Specifically, wherever a stroke is expected you can\0Apass any of the following values:\0A\0A- A length specifying the stroke's thickness. The color is inherited,\0A  defaulting to black.\0A- A color to use for the stroke. The thickness is inherited, defaulting to\0A  `{1pt}`.\0A- A stroke combined from color and thickness using the `+` operator as in\0A  `{2pt + red}`.\0A\0AFor full control, you can also provide a [dictionary] or a `{stroke}` object\0Ato any function that expects a stroke. The dictionary's keys may include any\0Aof the parameters for the constructor function, shown below.\0A\0A# Fields\0AOn a stroke object, you can access any of the fields listed in the\0Aconstructor function. For example, `{(2pt + blue).thickness}` is `{2pt}`.\0AMeanwhile, `{stroke(red).cap}` is `{auto}` because it's unspecified. Fields\0Aset to `{auto}` are inherited." }>, align 1
