@anon.9efbc0c128adab6190fabe7f7e045142.1084 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [907 x i8] }> <{ [907 x i8] c"A label for an element.\0A\0AInserting a label into content attaches it to the closest preceding element\0Athat is not a space. The preceding element must be in the same scope as the\0Alabel, which means that `[Hello #[<label>]]`, for instance, wouldn't work.\0A\0AA labelled element can be [referenced]($ref), [queried]($query) for, and\0A[styled]($styling) through its label.\0A\0AOnce constructed, you can get the name of a label using\0A[`str`]($str/#constructor).\0A\0A# Example\0A```example\0A#show <a>: set text(blue)\0A#show label(\22b\22): set text(red)\0A\0A= Heading <a>\0A*Strong* #label(\22b\22)\0A```\0A\0A# Syntax\0AThis function also has dedicated syntax: You can create a label by enclosing\0Aits name in angle brackets. This works both in markup and code. A label's\0Aname can contain letters, numbers, `_`, `-`, `:`, and `.`.\0A\0ACurrently, labels can only be attached to elements in markup mode, not in\0Acode mode. This might change in the future." }>, align 1
